Notes for maintainers (not part of the license; delete before publishing if
you prefer a clean file):

- This was reconstructed from memory of the public Functional Source
  License text (fsl.software, created by Sentry for exactly this
  "protect against hosted-service competitors now, convert to a
  standard permissive license later" scenario). Before publishing, pull
  the exact current canonical text from fsl.software and diff it against
  this file — treat this as a faithful-effort draft, not a verified
  transcription.
- Confirm "Checkmate Labs" is the correct legal entity name to hold
  copyright and be named Licensor.
- The License Key / circumvention clause is boilerplate from the standard
  FSL text; PwnBot doesn't currently have license-key-gated functionality,
  so it's inert today but costs nothing to leave in for future use (e.g. a
  managed-cloud tier). Remove it if it's confusing to reviewers.
- The four-year Change Date (2030-09-24) mirrors the common BUSL/CockroachDB
  default — shorten or lengthen based on how long you expect to need the
  competitive protection. FSL also has an official 2-year-default variant
  if four years feels too long for a fast-moving security product.
- Unlike Business Source License 1.1, FSL carries no third-party naming
  covenant, so pairing it with Apache-2.0 as the Change License (rather
  than a GPL-compatible license) is consistent — this avoids the
  inconsistency a BUSL draft would have had.
- This is a first draft for internal review, not legal advice — have an
  actual lawyer review it before this repository goes public or you rely
  on it commercially, especially the "for the avoidance of doubt" paragraph
  above, which I added to make the self-hosting-for-your-own-product
  permission explicit and is not part of Sentry's original template.
